# Workflow Rules

- At startup, discover and remember the branch or worktree assigned to your role.
- If your assigned worktree is `master`, work in the main project checkout on its current branch; do not expect or create a `.worktrees/<role>` directory for that role.
- Work only in your assigned branch or worktree.
- Do not inspect, diff, merge, or base work on another branch unless that branch is specifically named in a handoff or explicit user instruction.
- Use `./tmp/` in your assigned worktree for temporary files; do not use `/tmp`.
- Use `./swarmforge/scripts/swarm_handoff.sh <draft-file>` from your assigned checkout or worktree for handoffs between agents. Do not rely on PATH lookup. It sets `SWARMFORGE_ROLE` as the sender, so that variable must be set; it rejects a commit that is not in the repository and a priority that is not two digits.
- Maintain the tracked root `logbook.md` file for every handoff received or sent.
- When receiving a handoff, add a `logbook.md` note with a timestamp, the complete handoff message received, and a brief description of the action taken.
- When sending a handoff, add a `logbook.md` note with a timestamp, the complete handoff message sent, and a brief summary of the handoff contents.
- Commit `logbook.md` updates with the related work; do not leave handoff log entries untracked or uncommitted.
- For every handoff, write a draft file to `./tmp/<target-role>-handoff.txt` and send it with `./swarmforge/scripts/swarm_handoff.sh ./tmp/<target-role>-handoff.txt`. Do not pass handoff content directly as shell arguments.
- A `git_handoff` draft contains exactly these header lines and nothing else: `type: git_handoff`, `to: <role>[,<role>...]`, `priority: NN`, `task: <specifier handoff name>`, `commit: <10-char commit abbreviation>`.
- Do not write the handoff body. The tool writes it: `Re-read your role and constitution.` followed by `merge_and_process <sender> <commit>`. The sender is your `SWARMFORGE_ROLE`, so the draft carries no sender field.
- The specifier invents a short, stable handoff name for each accepted specification handoff.
- Every later handoff for that work must carry that name as its `task`.
- Handoffs report only essential state, never process. Anything a receiving role needs to know beyond the commit belongs in the tracked `logbook.md`, which travels with the merge.
- Do not tell the receiving role how to do its job, repeat your process, or ask it to continue sender-owned responsibilities.
- After receiving a handoff, merge the commit named in the handoff into your assigned branch before applying your own role rules. The commit identifies the state on its own; no branch name is needed and none is sent.
- When receiving a handoff, ignore sender process narrative and decide next actions only from your own role prompt, the constitution, and the current project state.
- When the architect sends a priority `00` follow-up to the coder or refactorer, it is an architect review loop, not a new specifier-originated implementation phase.
  - The receiving role must merge, review, and verify under its own role rules.
  - If no further role-owned changes are needed, hand the verified state back to the architect using the same task name instead of forwarding it to the specifier or the next normal pipeline role.
  - If the coder needs implementation changes for an architect follow-up, the coder may make and verify them, then hand the result to the refactorer as normal; after the refactorer completes, the result returns to the architect.
  - If the refactorer needs structural changes for an architect follow-up, the refactorer may make and verify them, then hand the result back to the architect.
  - The architect alone sends the phase-complete specifier sync after all coder/refactorer follow-up loops for the task are closed and accepted.
- If the architect's review finds a defect in Gherkin scenario content (acceptance criteria or examples, not pipeline registration or mutation-manifest housekeeping), the architect must not edit the feature file directly. Send a priority `00` follow-up to the specifier describing the finding instead, and treat the task as not yet advanceable until that loop returns.
  - The specifier reviews the finding under its own role rules and decides whether to accept, revise, or decline the proposed change, then commits its decision and hands the result back to the architect using the same task name.
  - The architect resumes the review only after this specifier loop returns and is accepted, the same way it waits for a coder or refactorer follow-up loop to close.
- If one or more messages arrive while you are busy:
  - Save each complete message as its own file in a local untracked `pending-messages/` directory in your assigned worktree.
  - Name queued message files with this format: `PP-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-source.txt`, where `PP` is the two-digit priority.
  - Use priority `00` for architect handoffs to coder, refactorer, or specifier.
  - Use priority `50` for normal messages unless a role prompt or explicit user instruction sets a different priority.
  - Finish the current job before acting on queued messages.
  - After the current job is complete and any required handoff has been sent, process queued message files in sorted filename order.
  - Before removing any queued handoff file, add a `logbook.md` note with a timestamp, the complete handoff message from that file, and the reason for the note.
  - Delete each queued message file only after processing it. Do not use `rm -f`; use a non-forced deletion command so queue cleanup does not trigger escalation.
- If the expected git layout or assigned worktree is missing, stop and report instead of silently working in the wrong place.
